00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.470
world, is it validated question.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:02.110
There are always going to
be getting is it validated?

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.170
But what is validation
can mean different things

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:06.190
to different people.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.290
Yes, there's an
official definition,

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:11.200
but it means different
things to different people.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.700
So the as I understand it,
the definition of validation

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.470
is establishing
documentary evidence

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.170
that our software performs some
sort of process, procedure,

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.960
activity and compliance
with our specifications

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.300
with a high degree of assurance.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.950
So in layman's terms,
we're creating documents

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.962
to prove that our software
does what we say it does.

00:01:30.962 --> 00:01:32.670
But why do we even
care about validation?

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.970
Why is that question even asked?

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.220
Well, a lot of people
refer to the fact

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:41.060
that we have to validate
our software for submission.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.832
But there's a lot of unspoken
benefits around validation

00:01:43.832 --> 00:01:45.290
that don't always
get talked about,

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.490
such as improved quality
and safety of our code.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.240
Because we've gone
through and checked it.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.530
We're very confident in
the quality of the code

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:54.380
as well as the fact that
they'll return proper results.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.750
It results in faster
processing because we already

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:57.480
have this code.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:58.110
Now set up.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:03.050
We can use it across multiple
projects and trust it'll work.

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:05.240
And then it also
promotes trust because we

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.040
perform this validation
we've gone through

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.410
and vetted it the
best to our abilities.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:10.470
So we are confident.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.050
Then when we give
it an input, it'll

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.200
give us a consistent output as
well as through an error when

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.910
things are go out of bounds.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.710
The validation, the practice
can be a really high bar.

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:22.590
And there's a lot
of documents that

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.640
go into creating validation
because first, you

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.410
have to fill out a form
for specifications,

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.800
plant uses, environments.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:30.967
You're going to be using it.

00:02:30.967 --> 00:02:32.490
And then write out
some of your code

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.600
based on the specifications,
record, the function

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.810
of authorship and some
external file, potentially

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.090
an Excel file, then
get another form

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.490
to document your test cases.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:41.950
Testing, testing, environment.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.100
How you plan on
doing your testing,

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.500
then may be the last form
to show how your testing

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:47.800
plan is comprehensive.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.220
That you're proving that all
your specifications are being

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.290
met through your testing plan.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:55.020
Have a third party come through
and manually evaluate your test

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.313
and get screenshots for results.

00:02:57.313 --> 00:02:59.230
And then you have to
review your documentation

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:01.990
and combine it into a final
validation packet for release.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:04.380
But information is shared
across all of these documents,

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.020
so any time one
of them updating,

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.270
go back and update it.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:09.245
And if you manually
evaluated all your tests

00:03:09.245 --> 00:03:10.620
and had to make
an update, you're

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.330
going to have to go
through rerun everything.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:13.770
So it's incredibly inefficient.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:14.800
And at any time.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.160
It can feel like game over
because you're just redoing

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.630
your work over and over again.

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:22.390
But today, I want to
tell you the validation

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:26.410
and can be best friends,
they can work together

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.430
to provide a
validation framework,

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:31.540
such that we can achieve
all the goals of validation

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:34.990
without the as much
additional stress and work

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:36.520
that can be involved with that.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:38.680
Using a combination
of R Markdown test

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.230
that and our oxygen
to, we can essentially

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:44.710
make validation push
button for validation.

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:47.530
And generate a document that
looks like this, where we're

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:51.820
able to capture a place
for signatures for everyone

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.138
that's been involved
with the project,

00:03:54.138 --> 00:03:56.680
we're able to have information
