00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.760
we performed our validation and
record, who wrote what pieces

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.550
of the project, the specs,
the functions, the test

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:06.850
cases, the code,
as well as share

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:10.760
what the coverage is between
our specs and our test cases.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.720
And finally record all
of our content here,

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.760
including our test
results, all at

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.620
the click of a button without
having to redo as much work.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:22.230
And this is the R Package
Validation framework,

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:25.500
so there's five key elements
to the framework that

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.900
is recording your specs,
writing your code,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.550
recording your test cases,
writing your test code.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:34.082
And finally generating
the documentation.

00:04:34.082 --> 00:04:35.790
The advantage of using
this framework is,

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:39.870
well, it's integrated into the
arcoxia development process.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.870
It goes it takes you from the
beginning of the ideation of I

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.550
want to create a
package all the way

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:47.460
through the process to creating
a package that now you've

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:48.300
validated.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.720
It's native to our programmers.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:50.820
It lives.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.340
This framework lives
within the package itself.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:56.430
So you don't have to leave your
R package environment in order

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.790
to form your validation or have
all the documents in there.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:01.350
It allows for
iterative development

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.120
because we don't
have docs living

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.130
across multiple locations.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:09.030
It it allows us to update pieces
without having to make sure

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.580
that we've updated the five
different other documents

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:13.170
with that piece of information.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:17.130
It's reusable and extensible
because it's reliant on code.

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.440
We're able to just
rerun things and make

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.720
sure it'll just run and work.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:22.720
And it's extensible.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.690
If you want to move
pieces out of your package

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.300
into more utility
focused package,

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:28.480
you can totally do that.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.020
And just copy out your
test cases and specs

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.680
and whatnot into
that new package.

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:36.240
And you don't have to be putting
in a whole new round of effort

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:37.943
into doing the.

00:05:37.943 --> 00:05:39.860
They're going to start
at the beginning, which

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:42.320
is specifications now,
specifications define

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:44.040
the expectations of a package.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.220
So you kind of think of
that as like the blueprints

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:47.110
for your package.

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:51.080
So it's really defining what
is the goal of your package?

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:53.975
A good specification
is it is going to have

00:05:53.975 --> 00:05:55.100
several different elements.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:56.767
It's like, what will
the thing be doing?

00:05:56.767 --> 00:05:58.140
What are the expected inputs?

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.750
What are the expected outputs?

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:02.390
What are warnings or errors
that should be triggered when

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:04.280
things like letting us
know, letting the user

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:07.190
know when things are going
out of bounds, but critically,

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:09.930
a specification will not
rely on external knowledge.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:11.840
You can assume that
there's basic knowledge

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:14.270
in the programmer
side of things,

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:16.550
but do not assume that they
have contextual knowledge

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:19.530
around the statistics
or the process.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:23.170
You may be trying to
create into your package.

