that need to be loading in,

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:46.502
if there's any
environment, variables

00:10:46.502 --> 00:10:48.703
need to be setting
that little detail.

00:10:48.703 --> 00:10:50.120
This is what you
need to be doing.

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:52.520
It'll be it's a
recipe, really, for how

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:55.340
to get from this set up
input into the desired

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:57.800
output with clear
expectations for what you're

00:10:57.800 --> 00:10:59.060
going to be testing against.

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:00.830
So if you're reading
in a data set,

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:03.170
you want to be clear to
say, rather than saying,

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:07.040
check the links, go check that
the length of this data set

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:09.170
is 34 rows.

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:11.240
But critically, there's
no code provided

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:14.450
because you want whoever is
going to be performing the test

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:17.360
testing to not be able to copy
and paste the code because then

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:20.403
you can actually test it.

00:11:20.403 --> 00:11:22.070
When you're documenting
your test cases,

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:24.470
we're going to capture the same
information that we have been

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:26.262
who wrote the tests
and when they wrote it,

00:11:26.262 --> 00:11:28.760
but we're also going to
capture, which specifications

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:31.890
are being satisfied because I
can update those tests, update.

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:33.240
And we can use wroxton tags.

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:34.670
Surprise, surprise to do that.

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:37.010
So at section less
updated by that section

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:41.820
of last updated date and at
section specification coverage.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:44.490
And so if I were to be writing
a test case for my presentation

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:47.230
today, I have my sections.

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:48.000
At the top here.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:49.650
So I wrote this test case.

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:50.650
This is what I wrote it.

00:11:50.650 --> 00:11:54.900
And these are the test case in
which specs are being covered.

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:57.870
I have a setup where I
create my presentation.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:01.560
Then I will go through and prove
that I met my specifications

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.300
by my first test test.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:05.980
The presentation was informative
by asking my audience

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:09.360
that what they learned, I
will tie my presentation,

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:11.670
make sure it's between
15 and 20 minutes long

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:14.820
and test it as entertaining by
counting the number of chuckles

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:17.250
from the audience, which
unfortunately, I can't hear you

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:17.750
right now.

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:20.370
Hopefully you're
laughing or when

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.740
I practice it to myself,
other the number of roles

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:25.120
and trust me, there
were a few of them.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:25.620
All right.

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:27.935
So now we have all
the setup here.

00:12:27.935 --> 00:12:30.310
So now we're going to fill in
the lines with test coding.

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:32.550
So test coding is the
actual implementation

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:34.420
of the test cases in code.

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:37.680
We're going to be then recording
the results to prove that we've

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:38.920
met our specifications.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:41.287
But very, very
importantly, a third party

00:12:41.287 --> 00:12:42.870
be doing this to
somebody that was not

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:45.240
involved in the
writing of the package

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:46.390
or the writing of the test.

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:49.090
Cases can now go through
and perform the testing.

00:12:49.090 --> 00:12:51.000
They could have written
the specifications,

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:53.853
but so the benefits of
doing this test coding

00:12:53.853 --> 00:12:55.770
is going to help resolve
interpretation errors

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:57.480
in your documentation
and examples,

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.040
because this person
doesn't actually

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.460
know how to use your package.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:03.960
They're able to they're going
to be using your documentation

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:06.810
to see how they're supposed
to be achieving the test

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:07.950
cases that you lay out.

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:10.470
And if they have any, they can
raise any questions about it,

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:11.790
about your documentation.

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:14.937
This wasn't very clear to me
because you're, as the author,

00:13:14.937 --> 00:13:16.770
intrinsically understand
how the function is

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:17.880
supposed to be behaving.

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:19.150
This new person
doesn't know that.

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:21.750
So they can ask you to improve
your documentation or examples.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:23.670
If the test case itself
isn't very clear,

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:25.120
they're going to come
to their own conclusion.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:26.480
And so then it's going to fail.

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:28.980
And they are able to you're
able to go back and then improve

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:29.940
your test cases.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.430
And the failing test case is
not necessarily a bad thing.

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:35.770
It just means that you need
to improve your documentation.

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:40.110
And then any interpretation
errors in your specifications.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.060
If they didn't write,
their specifications

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:44.650
will begin once they can go
back and help improve those.

00:13:44.650 --> 00:13:46.990
And then in general, it
helps identify improvements.

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:50.580
So if your code runs slowly or
if an argument is incredibly

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:53.063
clear, they're able
to go back and go,

00:13:53.063 --> 00:13:54.730
hey, we were missing
this argument here.

00:13:54.730 --> 00:13:56.287
Can you please add that in?

00:13:56.287 --> 00:13:57.870
As I was going through
the test cases,

00:13:57.870 --> 00:13:59.280
it didn't really make
a whole bunch of sense

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:01.170
to be able to have to
set it up this way.

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:03.690
So it really allows for
iterative improvement

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:06.152
before you even get
to the first release.

00:14:06.152 --> 00:14:08.110
So we're going to use a
combination of testthat

00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:11.465
that and surprise,
surprise or oxygen to.

