00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:19.910
by and last updated date.

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:21.670
And so this is an
example of what

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:23.590
the test code might look like.

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:26.820
So we have the header
here at the top.

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:30.130
And so each test piece
will have its own test.

00:14:30.130 --> 00:14:34.040
That section here, it's exactly
like any other test that.

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:35.900
But we have the slot
that last updated

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:39.140
by just her last updated date.

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:40.500
Here's the expectation.

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:43.010
The test, they have,
the code they run

00:14:43.010 --> 00:14:45.110
and the expectations
completely like

00:14:45.110 --> 00:14:47.610
any other test that set up.

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:50.040
And the reason we're using
that is a familiar framework

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:52.090
that's used by a ton
of packages on crack.

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:54.930
Right now, it's the de
facto standard basically

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:57.260
at this point for unit testing.

00:14:57.260 --> 00:15:00.725
So we're going to steal that
and use it for our purposes.

00:15:00.725 --> 00:15:02.850
It can also be run and
develop either interactively

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:03.580
or in batch.

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:06.210
So we don't have to have
somebody manually running it,

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:10.210
but they can manually run it as
they're building up their test.

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:13.660
But the real and the
big reason why we use

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:16.150
test that is for the reporter
objects that live within test

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:16.650
that.

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:20.860
So reporter objects are a
special live inside test

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:23.830
that helped track and
test each expectation

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:26.110
and reports,
whether they succeed

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:28.330
